{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"03975a8141495bfe78ea0f462942ac501870e34a2974c3d3fa368392db194059","slug":"index","location":"/chapters/ch00_overview/index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Multimedia Retrieval","content_includes_title":false,"thumbnail":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_2-3507480c9f7fbf1fc48e221713954a44.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/index-ccf2342f135298df6580a1b230000de1.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"About this book","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"sgwGgro1xC"}],"key":"olvp0x49cw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the companion textbook for ","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"gQSjDZnmBm"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multimedia Retrieval","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"epa9ZnOLCZ"}],"key":"BQ44R6Vi8z"},{"type":"text","value":", a Master’s-level course at the University of Basel, edition HS26. Course announcements, exercise submissions, and scheduling are managed through ","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"XLqaOi4m2g"},{"type":"link","url":"https://adam.unibas.ch/goto_adam_crs_2206931.html","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"ADAM","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"lGR7AONdGy"}],"urlSource":"https://adam.unibas.ch/goto_adam_crs_2206931.html","key":"zztFMKQmOK"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"Yx4R6ebeyG"},{"type":"link","url":"https://github.com/roger-weber/mmir-unibasel-hs26","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"GitHub","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"BKn88eKyek"}],"urlSource":"https://github.com/roger-weber/mmir-unibasel-hs26","error":true,"key":"RmOoDbKMWr"}],"key":"tSCZY7ygFC"}],"key":"xfDf1rFoyG"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"From Ctrl+F to AI Answers","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"cXJm2LBpox"}],"identifier":"from-ctrl-f-to-ai-answers","label":"From Ctrl+F to AI Answers","html_id":"from-ctrl-f-to-ai-answers","implicit":true,"key":"hgwrfXBl9v"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose you need to find all books about cats in a library. A simple string search (Ctrl+F for “cat”) scans every page until it finds a match. Even for a modest collection this is impractical, and the result is merely a Boolean filter: it tells you which books contain the string “cat”, but it cannot tell you which ones are ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"wjMI9bBdLH"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"most relevant","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"Ppqb88kkTV"}],"key":"e7eQFjuWiK"},{"type":"text","value":" to what you are looking for. For popular topics, you are left with a long list of matches, and have to manually browse through the results to find what you wanted.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"nsysBBQFT0"}],"key":"zJHkHMLzwr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retrieval systems solve this problem in two steps. First, they organize information in advance through preprocessing and indexing: extracting features from documents, building data structures that enable fast lookup, and storing statistics that describe each document’s content. Second, at query time they apply ranking models that score documents by their relevance to the query rather than simply filtering by presence or absence of a term.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"NbLIVcTaPy"}],"key":"DMBlHtgXkw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modern AI systems have made retrieval even more critical. Large language models can in theory process long documents, but doing so is expensive (costs scale with token count) and prone to hallucination when the model must rely on memorized knowledge. In practice, it is far more effective to first retrieve the relevant passages from a document collection and feed only those into the model’s context. This pattern, retrieval-augmented generation, depends entirely on the quality of the retrieval step: if the wrong passages are retrieved, the generated answer will be wrong too.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"MmU7QCLoCY"}],"key":"kYYNwx12zT"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Semantic Gap","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"Pid0oFXFMW"}],"label":"opening-semantic-gap","identifier":"opening-semantic-gap","html_id":"opening-semantic-gap","key":"YPL6ODQPgd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider searching for a picture of a cat. Nothing in the raw pixel values tells a machine that an image depicts a cat. The system sees a grid of numbers; the user asks a question about meaning. This mismatch between how humans describe what they want and how machines represent information is called the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"xWWpXzUtGk"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic gap","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"guaSWLuP7j"}],"key":"atETSQaUUT"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"Tb1oi6o85B"}],"key":"cvCnsF9iKw"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_2-3507480c9f7fbf1fc48e221713954a44.png","alt":"A human sees a “cat”; a machine sees a grid of binary values.","width":"70%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"tycXwlmZxv","urlSource":"images/figure_0_2.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"fig-semantic-gap","identifier":"fig-semantic-gap","html_id":"fig-semantic-gap","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"z7yzfxmTYs"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"YUdSsIKKQ2"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"KmbKz3qiLS"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"jvUL1ksEbY"},{"type":"text","value":"A human sees a “cat”; a machine sees a grid of binary values.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"IdZY6B2w85"}],"key":"jTPiETaAYG"}],"key":"vDY5Cps0Yr"}],"label":"fig-semantic-gap","identifier":"fig-semantic-gap","enumerator":"1","html_id":"fig-semantic-gap","key":"cR5fwoVEVR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The gap exists in text too, though it is narrower. A search for “cat” will match many documents containing that word or its plural “cats”, but it will miss documents that discuss “felines” or mention specific breeds by name without ever using the word “cat”. In images, audio, and video the gap is far wider: there is no surface-level token to match against.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"je5hx3ZPLx"}],"key":"nKdZcZ7NXt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The semantic gap plagued earlier retrieval systems, especially those working with non-text media. Recent breakthroughs in AI, particularly dense embeddings and transformer models, have dramatically narrowed it by learning to map both queries and documents into a shared space where meaning, not surface form, determines similarity. This course traces the full path from systems that match keywords to systems that understand meaning.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"Bs9wlkUVmv"}],"key":"EzfHSenZ2s"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Closing the Gap","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"eBpEj7m0n2"}],"identifier":"closing-the-gap","label":"Closing the Gap","html_id":"closing-the-gap","implicit":true,"key":"YMNND7LsAh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The semantic gap is not one problem but a spectrum of problems, and this course attacks them in order of increasing sophistication.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"zeFEm05NZ1"}],"key":"Pqiiv3qCQu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first step is keyword matching: building an index so that a search for “cat” instantly finds every document containing that word, without scanning the entire collection. This alone closes the gap for queries where the user’s words appear verbatim in the target. But as we saw, many relevant documents use different vocabulary.","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"lVLbgUXkja"}],"key":"stVlfAgfSA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second step is statistical ranking. Instead of treating every keyword match equally, we weight terms by how informative they are and score documents by how well they match the query overall. A document that mentions “cat” once in passing ranks lower than one dedicated to the topic. This narrows the gap further, but only within the space of shared vocabulary.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"qVfkhSSVIz"}],"key":"OywpXJIuoV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The third step is semantic matching. We represent both queries and documents as dense vectors in a shared space, trained so that “cat” and “feline” end up near each other. Now the system can find relevant documents even when no words overlap. This is where the gap narrows dramatically for text, and where non-text media (images, audio) first become searchable by meaning.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"liEWoINr9F"}],"key":"AmDvAMDccW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The final step is retrieval-augmented generation. Rather than returning a ranked list of documents, the system retrieves the most relevant passages and feeds them to a language model that synthesizes a direct answer. The gap between question and answer is closed end-to-end.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"y5GoIX9wml"}],"key":"y8rHDkEnzv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each step builds on the previous one. A RAG pipeline still needs an index (step 1), still benefits from good ranking (step 2), and works best with semantic retrieval (step 3). This is why the book is structured as a cumulative progression rather than a menu of independent topics.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"h4rJl4uYpz"}],"key":"v0IEJBEH3M"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From card catalogues to transformers: six decades of retrieval (optional reading)","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"ujhVWOW8yB"}],"key":"x8iMmqpDyG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 1960s","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"eQb5NVFBap"}],"key":"Tl8s8yh1Nd"},{"type":"text","value":" saw the birth of computerized information retrieval. Calvin Mooers had coined the term “information retrieval” in 1950, but serious research began only in the 1960s. H. P. Luhn created KWIC (Key Word In Context) indexes, IBM developed the STAIRS system, and Gerard Salton began work on SMART at Cornell. Most systems remained experimental because few texts existed in machine-readable form.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"M3E9mpKFQx"}],"key":"BcdI6LO1xp"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_3-5465b5394db155e99171710ca4d87ca6.png","width":"80%","key":"aHLoQhOUAX","urlSource":"images/figure_0_3.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-1960","identifier":"fig-ir-history-1960","enumerator":"2","html_id":"fig-ir-history-1960","key":"pIUAyDNh9f"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 1970s","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"iaha1toAQo"}],"key":"ocCNBTkmA0"},{"type":"text","value":" brought the first practical systems. Widespread computer typesetting produced the machine-readable text needed for large-scale retrieval. Salton’s vector space model represented documents and queries as vectors whose similarity could be measured mathematically. Robertson and Sparck Jones developed the probabilistic framework that would later lead to BM25. Commercial systems like Lockheed Dialog and MEDLARS demonstrated that automated retrieval could work at scale.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"SQqgAqS6LD"}],"key":"OD5jTT0mI9"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_4-bf8393aed007f7a45140d5964b1c08b1.png","width":"80%","key":"hFmD1gYOtS","urlSource":"images/figure_0_4.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-1970","identifier":"fig-ir-history-1970","enumerator":"3","html_id":"fig-ir-history-1970","key":"dEN7XEpuwL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 1980s","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"XjzebN4u2a"}],"key":"WMmnwmQJGX"},{"type":"text","value":" refined these models. Boolean retrieval became the dominant commercial approach, letting users combine terms with AND, OR, and NOT. Robertson formalized the Probability Ranking Principle. The Okapi system at City University London gave its name to the BM25 ranking function. Latent Semantic Indexing appeared as a method to reveal hidden relationships between terms.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"Plo0Urm9K7"}],"key":"XQjN8cm0mo"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_5-bc48bf7c89abc8396a3f311339ab053d.png","width":"80%","key":"rtrWqE8fiG","urlSource":"images/figure_0_5.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-1980","identifier":"fig-ir-history-1980","enumerator":"4","html_id":"fig-ir-history-1980","key":"LMpzgPC6eg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 1990s","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"KSJ3kiW8hH"}],"key":"kPZ5kf61pO"},{"type":"text","value":" brought the web revolution. Early search engines (Archie, WebCrawler, AltaVista) appeared to help users navigate the new online world. The most significant development was PageRank (Page and Brin, 1996), which ranked pages by the authority of incoming links rather than keyword matching alone.","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"MNDl5vXU5g"}],"key":"LVhG59pi5o"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_6-0cd0455d5c0085e5cdcdb3f11b698f55.png","width":"80%","key":"WfvVXwp1Ha","urlSource":"images/figure_0_6.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-1990","identifier":"fig-ir-history-1990","enumerator":"5","html_id":"fig-ir-history-1990","key":"N1kPGAbidC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 2000s","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"key":"gE0jO9kSUd"}],"key":"Q7Os9SIGoJ"},{"type":"text","value":" introduced machine learning into ranking. Learning to Rank (LTR) trained supervised models to order results by relevance. BM25 became a standard baseline. Deep learning re-emerged after a quiet period, driven by more powerful GPUs and larger datasets.","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"key":"Rbjz46vbWF"}],"key":"AdjITilpp5"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_7-87393da0e97f0bd80efaa650782c059c.png","width":"80%","key":"HhrWlnRP0e","urlSource":"images/figure_0_7.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-2000","identifier":"fig-ir-history-2000","enumerator":"6","html_id":"fig-ir-history-2000","key":"CtcvBuxSYZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 2010s","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"nPgXDbol3b"}],"key":"MmvBBv05mr"},{"type":"text","value":" saw deep learning applied directly to retrieval. Neural ranking models replaced handcrafted features with learned representations. Google introduced BERT in 2018, enabling search engines to understand context and word relationships. Transformer architectures fundamentally changed how machines handle sequential data.","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"ewkdeh61Wc"}],"key":"njs0l3KIAq"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_8-ad6c7cb84502298b3d3d52a2bc182c23.png","width":"80%","key":"Z6Cqprjask","urlSource":"images/figure_0_8.png"}],"label":"fig-ir-history-2010","identifier":"fig-ir-history-2010","enumerator":"7","html_id":"fig-ir-history-2010","key":"aUYmHur2or"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 2020s","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"iOWbcy7X8e"}],"key":"k0GcfyT1W4"},{"type":"text","value":" brought semantic search and vector databases as production infrastructure. Retrieval-Augmented Generation combines retrieval with large language models. Agentic systems can reason about information needs, execute multi-step retrieval, and synthesize results from multiple sources.","position":{"start":{"line":96,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"key":"l78k0ySq04"}],"key":"EP8bC1Rjm0"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/figure_0_9-c9e887d42572dd2ef2ae6c614df8d700.png","alt":"Three architectures for knowledge-grounded AI: a RAG pipeline with vector retrieval (left), a conversational interface (center), and an agentic system selecting among tools and knowledge sources (right).","width":"90%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"NYENuW3imp","urlSource":"images/figure_0_9.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":103,"column":1},"end":{"line":103,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","identifier":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","html_id":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","enumerator":"8","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"gI6C28NXoJ"},{"type":"text","value":"8","key":"OIqp4Lhi93"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"nBuxDrAbB7"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"DEbhZhEqj5"},{"type":"text","value":"Three architectures for knowledge-grounded AI: a RAG pipeline with vector retrieval (left), a conversational interface (center), and an agentic system selecting among tools and knowledge sources (right).","position":{"start":{"line":103,"column":1},"end":{"line":103,"column":1}},"key":"Vo24D7bT16"}],"key":"Y8OTVdXUVn"}],"key":"xN0mB2ku3f"}],"label":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","identifier":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","enumerator":"8","html_id":"fig-rag-agentic-overview","key":"hwLYRLO3aO"}],"key":"vYOlDnPmUe"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1},"end":{"line":110,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How This Book Is Organized","position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1},"end":{"line":110,"column":1}},"key":"ChGwdsnLBO"}],"identifier":"how-this-book-is-organized","label":"How This Book Is Organized","html_id":"how-this-book-is-organized","implicit":true,"key":"HhPumWMvBL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":112,"column":1},"end":{"line":112,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The book is structured in three parts that build on each other. Each chapter focuses on one retrieval capability and the techniques to implement it. The path is cumulative: later chapters assume you have worked through the earlier ones.","position":{"start":{"line":112,"column":1},"end":{"line":112,"column":1}},"key":"jffiVFvvGa"}],"key":"MAsSdbFiBV"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":114,"column":1},"end":{"line":114,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Part I: Foundations (Chapters 1-3)","position":{"start":{"line":114,"column":1},"end":{"line":114,"column":1}},"key":"d8AtFdGvwW"}],"identifier":"part-i-foundations-chapters-1-3","label":"Part I: Foundations (Chapters 1-3)","html_id":"part-i-foundations-chapters-1-3","implicit":true,"key":"dEJ309dSOq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first three chapters establish the fundamentals that every retrieval system relies on.","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"dd9I6vqNud"}],"key":"YbAhtxg9DB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column":1},"end":{"line":118,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column":1},"end":{"line":118,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 1: Classical Text Retrieval","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column":1},"end":{"line":118,"column":1}},"key":"Lbe2U66Lcw"}],"key":"inJJsvaZRb"},{"type":"text","value":" introduces the core problem: given a query and a collection of documents, find the relevant ones. We build from Boolean retrieval (exact keyword matching) through TF-IDF weighting to BM25, the statistical ranking model that still powers production search engines today.","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column":1},"end":{"line":118,"column":1}},"key":"Z99p0KnGlx"}],"key":"tku8V29iCT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 2: Performance Evaluation","position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"key":"QHFMtE8RU2"}],"key":"wPdsm771KN"},{"type":"text","value":" asks the question every retrieval engineer must answer: does this system actually work? We cover precision, recall, ranked evaluation metrics (MAP, NDCG), and the experimental methodology for benchmarking retrieval systems.","position":{"start":{"line":120,"column":1},"end":{"line":120,"column":1}},"key":"SAlXlnxL3Y"}],"key":"JnlNusCXSX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":122,"column":1},"end":{"line":122,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position"